```
@id("read-only-lookups")
permit (principal, action in [
    Drupe::Action::"get_workorder",
    Drupe::Action::"authority_get_approval",
    Drupe::Action::"list_workorders",
    Drupe::Action::"get_workorder_tasks",
    Drupe::Action::"get_workorder_costs",
    Drupe::Action::"get_workorder_actuals_vs_planned",
    Drupe::Action::"get_workorder_kpis",
    Drupe::Action::"get_schedule_calendar",
    Drupe::Action::"get_my_assigned_workorders",
    Drupe::Action::"get_failure_codes"
  ], resource);

@id("close-main-only-after-human-signoff")
permit (principal, action == Drupe::Action::"close_workorder", resource)
when { context.input.site_id == "MAIN" }
when temporal {
  formerly within 1h Drupe::Action::"get_workorder"::response{
    eventResource: resource,
    input.wonum: context.input.wonum,
    input.site_id: context.input.site_id,
    output.approval_outcome: "SIGNED_OFF",
    output.approver_type: "OPERATOR"
  }
};
```

I used the `get_workorder` response as the approval evidence, because it is the only source whose approver values are declared enums: `SIGNED_OFF` for an approval, and `OPERATOR` for a person. Declined or queued approvals, and approvals by `BOT` or `SCHEDULER`, do not match. The `authority_get_approval` output has free-form string fields (`decision`, `approver_kind`), so I could not safely match on its values. It is still allowed as a read-only lookup, but a call to it does not satisfy the close condition.

The close permit also requires the same `wonum` and `site_id`, an event within the last hour, and a completed `get_workorder` response in this session. A denied or failed call is recorded only as an error event and cannot satisfy it.

There is no permit for `approve_workorder` or for any other mutating tool, so those stay denied.
